# Equinox Business Rules

## Document Chain

The document chain is:

Sales Order
→ Invoice
→ ATW / Delivery Receipt
→ Withdrawal Slip

## Sales Order

A Sales Order contains:

* Customer
* Delivery Address
* Order Date
* Term
* Reference Number
* Order Type
* Sales Employee
* Items
* Quantities
* Unit Prices
* Remarks
* Status

Sales Order Items must reference an Item Master record.

## Invoice

An Invoice is created from a Sales Order.

The system must copy the relevant Sales Order information into the Invoice.

Customer and item information that is intended to be historical must be stored as a transaction snapshot.

An Invoice may contain only quantities that remain available from the originating Sales Order.

### Quantity Rule

For every Sales Order Item:

Remaining Invoice Quantity =
Sales Order Quantity
--------------------

Total quantities already invoiced for that Sales Order Item

The system must reject an Invoice quantity greater than the remaining quantity.

Multiple Invoices are allowed for one Sales Order.

## ATW / Delivery Receipt

An ATW/DR is created from an Invoice.

It may contain only quantities remaining from the Invoice.

### Quantity Rule

Remaining ATW/DR Quantity =
Invoice Quantity
----------------

Total quantities already allocated to ATW/DR documents

The system must reject an ATW/DR quantity greater than the remaining quantity.

Multiple ATW/DR documents are allowed for one Invoice.

## Withdrawal Slip

A Withdrawal Slip is created from an ATW/DR.

There must be exactly zero or one Withdrawal Slip associated with an ATW/DR.

Once an ATW/DR has a Withdrawal Slip, another Withdrawal Slip must not be created.

### Critical Rule

The one-Withdrawal-Slip-per-ATW/DR rule must not depend only on the UI.

It must be enforced server-side and preferably at the database level.

## Status

Use explicit status transitions.

Do not allow arbitrary status changes.

Suggested Sales Order statuses (approved):

* draft
* open
* closed
* cancelled

Suggested Invoice statuses (approved):

* draft
* posted
* cancelled

Suggested ATW/DR statuses (approved):

* draft
* released
* cancelled

Suggested Withdrawal Slip statuses (approved):

* draft
* issued
* cancelled

Master data / user status: `active`, `inactive`.

## Cancellation

Cancellation must not physically delete transactional documents.

Cancellation should:

* change status
* record cancellation user
* record cancellation timestamp
* record cancellation reason

## Editing

Editing permissions depend on document status and user role.

Once a downstream document exists, upstream transactional data must be protected from changes that would invalidate the downstream documents.

Never allow a user to change quantities in an upstream document in a way that causes:

* Invoice quantity > SO quantity
* ATW/DR quantity > Invoice quantity
* Withdrawal Slip quantity > ATW/DR quantity

## Master Data

Customers and Items are master data.

Master-data changes must not rewrite historical transaction snapshots.

## ATW and Delivery Receipt

Unless later requirements establish materially different behavior, treat ATW and Delivery Receipt as one document subsystem with a `document_type` field.

Possible values:

* ATW
* DR

Do not create separate duplicated business logic for ATW and DR unless required.
